NAME
	fsshield - Limit a new process's access to the filesystem

SYNOPSIS
	fsshield [-a file] ... [-r file] ... [-t file] ... [-nR] utility [argument] ...

DESCRIPTION
	The fsshield utility runs a specified utility, but limits it's access to
	the filesystem. By default it gives read-only access to some fundamental
	directories and /etc/passwd, access to the current working directory, and
	clean tmpfs mounts on /tmp and /var/tmp.

	In this man page, "read-only" means that a bind-mount with the read-only
	option is created. Other accessible files are normal bind-mounts. The
	permissions on the files are not modified.

OPTIONS
	The following options are supported:

	-a file
		Make file accessible.

	-r file
		Make file accessible but read-only.

	-t file
		Mount a tmpfs on file.

	-n
		Do not apply the preset file accesses.

	-R
		Make the current working directory read-only.

	The order of options do not matter, however -a overrides -r and -t,
	while -r overrides -t. The preset files accesses are
	-t /tmp -t /var/tmp -r /var/empty -r /etc/passwd -r /usr -r /bin
	-r /sbin -r /lib -r /lib64 -r /lib32 -r /libexec, however limited
	to files that exist (including broken symlinks). Additionally
	the current working directory is always accessible (-a ., or
	if -R is used: -r .), regardless of the use of -n.

OPERANDS
	The following operands are supported:

	utility
		The name of the utility to be invoked.

	argument
		A string to pass as an argument for the invoked utility.

EXIT STATUS
	If utility is invoked, the exit status of fsshield is the exit status
	of utility; otherwise, the fsshield utility exits with one of the
	following values:

	125    An error occurred in the fsshield utility.

	126    The utility specified by utility was found but could not be invoked.

	127    The utility specified by utility could not be found.

NOTES
	The files can be any type of file, including directories, not just
	regular files.

SEE ALSO
	None.
